---
import GardenLayout from "../layouts/GardenLayout.astro";
const enabled =
  import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_ENABLED === "true" &&
  /^phc_[A-Za-z0-9_]+$/.test(import.meta.env.PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY ?? "");
---

<GardenLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="How this digital garden handles analytics and browser privacy signals."
>
  <header class="page-intro">
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p>A small website, with limited audience measurement.</p>
  </header>
  <div class="article-grid about-content">
    <div class="section-label">Website privacy</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is Lukas Bühler’s digital garden. There are no visitor accounts,
        comment forms, or advertising trackers in the site.
      </p>
      <h2>Appearance preference</h2>
      <p>
        The site follows your system colour theme by default. If you choose
        Light or Dark in the footer, it saves that choice in your browser’s
        local storage. Choosing System removes the saved preference. This
        setting uses no cookies and is not sent to an analytics service.
      </p>
      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      {enabled ? (
        <p>
          On lukasbuehler.ch, I use PostHog’s EU service to understand which
          pages are read. Analytics is disabled on local previews and other
          hostnames.
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          PostHog analytics is currently disabled in this build. The following
          explains the cookieless configuration prepared for this website.
        </p>
      )}
      <p>
        When enabled, the site sends a pageview containing the public page
        address and technical event information to PostHog’s EU endpoint. It
        excludes query parameters, URL fragments, referral information, and page
        contents. Click tracking, session recordings, user profiles, and
        automatic error capture are disabled.
      </p>
      <p>
        PostHog is configured not to persist analytics data in cookies, local
        storage, or session storage. Its cookieless service receives the
        connection’s IP address and browser user agent and uses them, together
        with the site hostname and a daily changing salt, to calculate a visitor
        hash. This supports daily audience counts without a persistent
        identifier stored in your browser. Cookieless does not mean that no data
        is processed.
      </p>
      <h2>Your browser’s privacy choices</h2>
      <p>
        If your browser sends Do Not Track or Global Privacy Control, the site
        does not load or initialize PostHog and sends no analytics events. You
        can enable either supported signal in your browser or use a content
        blocker. Browsers differ in which signals they support.
      </p>
      <p>
        These preferences do not prevent the hosting provider from handling the
        network requests needed to deliver the page.
      </p>
      <h2>Further information</h2>
      <p>
        PostHog describes its{" "}
        <a href="https://posthog.com/tutorials/cookieless-tracking">
          cookieless measurement
        </a>{" "}
        and <a href="https://posthog.com/privacy">privacy practices</a>. An
        ordinary link to another website only takes you there when you choose to
        follow it; this site does not automatically load the linked PK Spot app
        or social media embeds.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</GardenLayout>
